behind a phase that builds the reactor's modules, with a v4 test on a reactor whose module depends\n      on its sibling.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-03T073952\n    date: \"2026-10-03\"\n    title: \"A state request on a Node project whose scripts.test names a runner 495 cannot read stops at verification_design with no survey, so the quality requirement is never named a blind spot\"\n    severity: low\n    priority: p3\n    scope: application\n    what_happened: >\n      When `scripts.test` names a runner 495 cannot read (`tsc && node --test`, or the `echo ... && exit 1` that\n      `npm init` writes), `detectNodeStack` in `src/application/stacks/node.ts` declares no control and only that\n      refusal under `capability_missing`. `verification-design.ts` then throws CAPABILITY_MISSING before any survey is\n      produced: a quality requirement such as QLT-01 is not named a blind spot, neither \"the project declares ESLint\n      itself\" for a project that declares it, nor any reason for a project that declares nothing, and the runner is\n      the only reason the owner reads.\n    what_expected: >\n      A state request on such a project ends on a survey that names each requirement it cannot measure with its own\n      reason, the unreadable runner among them, instead of stopping on the runner alone.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Take a Node project with a package-lock.json, `scripts.test` set to `tsc && node --test`, and `eslint` as a devDependency.\"\n      - \"Ask `/495 state` about the quality of the code: the run stops in verification_design on CAPABILITY_MISSING naming the runner, and no survey exists.\"\n      - \"With a readable runner, the same project's survey names QLT-01 a blind spot because the project declares ESLint itself.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      A detection with no control is refused as a whole at verification_design, before the survey that would name\n      the requirements left unmeasured; the stop on an unreadable runner predates the Node quality referential.\n    evidence: >\n      Probed in the review of e10s03 (no pending decision, no install, project digest unchanged, stop on\n      capability_missing); the same stop on an unreadable runner exists on main. No adoption could be offered there\n      anyway: the analysers it installs could never be declared without a runner.\n    risk_level: low\n    follow_ups: >\n      Let a state request on a target with no declarable control still end on a survey of blind spots, with a v2 test\n      on a Node project whose scripts.test is refused.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-03T134500\n    date: \"2026-10-03\"\n    title: \"Two changes of one dossier whose candidates are identical collide on the verification's idempotency key, so the second is blocked\"\n    severity: medium\n    priority: p2\n    scope: application\n    what_happened: >\n      `verify` in `src/application/phases/verify.ts` keys its operation `verify:<manifest digest>:<revision>`, and the\n      ledger refuses a second operation that holds the same key (`src/adapters/storage-sqlite/ledger.ts`, \"already holds\n      the idempotency key\"). The key names neither the change nor the program, while the operations of every change of a\n      dossier share one table: a second change whose candidate has the same manifest at the same revision is refused as a\n      repeat of the first and stays blocked.\n    what_expected: >\n      Each change verifies its own candidate, whatever another change of the dossier produced; a repeat is recognised only\n      within the change that ran it.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Adopt a trajectory of two increments in one dossier and let both increments produce the same candidate tree.\"\n      - \"Verify the first increment's candidate, close it, start the second and verify: the ledger refuses the operation on the reused key.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      The idempotency key is scoped to the candidate and a per-change revision counter, not to the change.\n    evidence: >\n      Named by the automated arbitration of e10s04 from its acceptance run, which noted it predates the branch and that a\n      multi-increment program makes it easier to reach; the key's composition is read in `verify.ts:37`. Not reproduced\n      outside that run.\n    risk_level: medium\n    follow_ups: >\n      Put the change id in the key, with a v2 test of two changes of one dossier verifying the same candidate.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-03T134600\n    date: \"2026-10-03\"\n    title: \"A trajectory document the kernel refuses stays in the object store, filed under a change that is never created\"\n    severity: low\n    priority: p3\n    scope: application\n    what_happened: >\n      `adopt` in `src/application/harness.ts` stores the trajectory document as an artifact of a freshly drawn change id\n      before `commitProgram` submits the program to the kernel. When the kernel refuses the trajectory (an unassigned\n      global requirement, an increment with no closing criterion), neither the program nor the change is written, but the\n      stored document remains, attached to a change id no record names.\n    what_expected: >\n      A refused trajectory leaves nothing behind, or what it leaves is named by the refusal.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Run `/495 adopt` on a trajectory whose global requirement no increment carries, no milestone verifies and no scope decision sets aside.\"\n      - \"The adoption is refused, no program or change exists, and the object store holds the document under the unused change id.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      The artifact is stored before the kernel decides, and nothing removes or names it after a refusal.\n    evidence: >\n      Named by the automated arbitration of e10s04 from its acceptance run (dossiers c2 and c4); the order of operations is\n      read in `harness.ts` (`artifacts.store` of the trajectory before `commitProgram`).\n    risk_level: low\n    follow_ups: >\n      Store the document only once the kernel has accepted the program, or record the refusal with the document it read.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-03T193000\n    date: \"2026-10-03\"\n    title: \"PMD is never qualified on a project that already carries more than 1000 violations, so a survey of exactly the legacy project a remediation targets is refused with a misleading reason\"\n    severity: medium\n    priority: p2\n    scope: application\n    what_happened: >\n      The PMD and CPD readers keep the first 1000 findings of a report (`MAX_QUALITY_FINDINGS`,\n      `src/adapters/execution/parsers.ts`). `witnessFindings` in `src/application/qualification.ts` judges the negative\n      witness failed only when one of the kept findings sits in a file the witness wrote. On a project whose own\n      violations fill the first 1000, the witness's violation is cut, the negative witness is read as passing, pmd is not\n      qualified, and G2 refuses the survey with a reason about the sensor, not about the truncation.\n    what_expected: >\n      The witnesses are judged on the whole report, or a truncation that could hide a witness's finding is named as such.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Take a Maven project whose sources carry more than 1000 PMD violations, adopt the quality referential and ask for a quality survey.\"\n      - \"The pmd control is not qualified at G2, although PMD detects the negative witness's violation.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      The witness judgement reads the truncated finding list, while the total count is kept only as a note.\n    evidence: >\n      Named by the automated arbitration of e10s05 from its acceptance run, as predating the branch; the cut and the\n      judgement are read in `parsers.ts:49-64` and `qualification.ts:89-104`.\n    risk_level: medium\n    follow_ups: >\n      Judge the witnesses before truncating, or keep every finding located in a witness file, with a test on a report of\n      more than 1000 findings whose witness finding comes last.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-03T193100\n    date: \"2026-10-03\"\n    title: \"In a French status, the explanation of a program's milestone stays in English\"\n    severity: low\n    priority: p3\n    scope: presentation\n    what_happened: >\n      The status of a program shown in French renders the milestone's explanation (what remains before it passes) in\n      English.\n    what_expected: >\n      Every line of a status is in the session's language.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Adopt a trajectory in a French session and run `/495 status` before the milestone passes.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      Not located: the explanation is likely written by the kernel's evaluation and shown untranslated.\n    evidence: >\n      Named by the automated arbitration of e10s05 from its acceptance run; not reproduced outside it.\n    risk_level: low\n    follow_ups: >\n      Find where the status takes the milestone explanation and translate it like the other status lines.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-03T220000\n    date: \"2026-10-03\"\n    title: \"A second survey of the same tree is blocked on the survey's idempotency key, so an owner who refuses the survey of the integrated project cannot take another without changing the tree\"\n    severity: medium\n    priority: p2\n    scope: application\n    what_happened: >\n      `survey` in `src/application/phases/survey.ts` keys its operation `survey:<reference digest>:<revision>`, a key that\n      names neither the change nor the program. A second state request on an unchanged tree reaches the same key, and the\n      ledger refuses it with OPERATION_ACTIVE. Since a remediation milestone is now judged on an accepted survey of the\n      integrated project, an owner who refuses that survey cannot measure the program again until the tree changes.\n    what_expected: >\n      Each state request runs its own survey; a repeat is recognised only within the change that ran it.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Integrate the increments of a remediation program, run `/495 state` on the integrated project and refuse the survey.\"\n      - \"Run `/495 state` again on the same tree: the second survey stops on OPERATION_ACTIVE for the reused key.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      The idempotency key is scoped to the reference tree and a per-change revision counter, not to the change. Same\n      shape as the verification key of BUG-2026-10-03T134500.\n    evidence: >\n      Named by the automated arbitration of e10s06 from its acceptance run; the key's composition is read in\n      `survey.ts:31`. Not reproduced outside that run.\n    risk_level: medium\n    follow_ups: >\n      Put the change id in the key, with the same fix as the verification key, and a test of two surveys of one tree.\n    status: open\nsrc/application/preparation.ts:100: * behaviour the reference does not have appears or not, so it can never decide a requirement asking\nsrc/application/context.ts:141:\t\t\t\t\t\t? 'You clarify and specify: separate facts, reversible assumptions, material questions, out-of-scope items and risks. Do not invent requirements that the request does not support; ask a material question instead. Give each requirement the category that says what a control measures of it, one of: \"functional\" for the behaviour the code shows; \"quality\" for complexity, duplication, dead code and style; \"coverage\" for the code the tests exercise; \"mutation\" for the mutants the tests kill; \"architecture\" for the structure and the dependencies between modules. A category the harness does not read leaves the requirement a blind spot. Set satisfied_by_reference to true only for a requirement the project already honours today, such as behaviour a refactoring must preserve; a requirement asking for something the tree does not do yet is false, and the harness will have a failing test written for it first. When the objective carries answered questions, each one marked `to declare` must appear in `answers`: name the mandatory requirements that carry the answer, and set observable to false only when the answer fixes nothing a control could observe — no status, no message, no bound. Saying nothing about such an answer is refused. An answer already declared is carried over for you: keep the requirements named beside it, or declare it again in `answers` if your requirements no longer hold it. Setting observable to false is a proposal, not a decision you make: it dispenses the answer from every requirement only once the change owner closes the question, and until then the change stops for the owner to confirm or refuse it.'\nsrc/application/installation.ts:37:\t\t\t\t`org.apache.maven.plugins:maven-dependency-plugin:${MAVEN_DEPENDENCY_PLUGIN_VERSION}:resolve-plugins`,\nsrc/application/phases/clarify.ts:88:\t\t// asking the owner to confirm the proposal, not merely restating a binding it dropped (BES-02).\nsrc/presentation/tui/review/diff-view.ts:8: * distinguish old from new without asking the reader to see a colour.\nsrc/presentation/tui/review/diff-view.ts:25: * loader that would. Asking for one here is what makes the change draw under Pi, under `node --test`\nsrc/presentation/tui/review/diff-view.ts:58: * than asking the renderer for less context: what is hidden is then exactly what the record calls\ntest/v4-platform/maven-quality.test.ts:45:\t\texecFileSync(\"mvn\", [\"-B\", \"-q\", \"org.apache.maven.plugins:maven-dependency-plugin:3.11.0:resolve-plugins\"], {\ntest/v4-platform/maven-quality-reactor.test.ts:79:\t\texecFileSync(\"mvn\", [\"-B\", \"-q\", \"org.apache.maven.plugins:maven-dependency-plugin:3.11.0:resolve-plugins\"], {","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"13ac641e-7432-4f95-bd80-f56aacab5d58","uuid":"7d981f30-8e64-4198-83c0-1cee86d990d2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T20:43:14.517Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-03T060500\n    date: \"2026-10-03\"\n    ti